  "query_preview": "..The mid/upper-level trough with a closed low at its\nbase is starting to push into Arizona as tropical moisture \ncontinues to sit across northern Arizona. Meanwhile, Hurricane \nPolo moving across Baja California. Radar shows heavier showers \nand thunderstorms are developing across eastern AZ, and this will\nbe pushing W to NW across the State today as the trough and close\nlow move eastward today. This will support impactful weather \nacross northern AZ today, with a greater focus over eastern AZ \nthis evening and Wednesday.\n\nFrom this morning through Wednesday morning, the low is forecast \nto move across northern Arizona. As it does so, additional \natmospheric moisture from Hurricane Polo will be entrained into \nthe low. With increased synoptic-scale forcing associated with the\npassing low, greater storm activity and higher rainfall rates are\nforecast. High-resolution model guidance continue to highlight \nthe eastern Mogollon Rim and White Mountains as the area with the \nhighest flood risk with 24 hour rainfall totals up to 3 inches. An\nanother signature of an inch or greater is starting to appear in\nthe 3-hr QPF HREF guidance near the UT and AZ border, near Lake\nPowell, this afternoon and early evening hours so this will be a \ntrend to watch. We will also see higher 0-6km Bulk Wind Shear, \nwith values ranging from 40-60 kts. Despite the strong shear, \nforecast CAPE values at or below 600 J/kg may limit the overall \nsevere thunderstorm threat, though isolated severe storms cannot \nbe ruled out given the benefit of forcing from the low. So, all \nthings considered, we are looking at a moderate/high risk of \nflooding/flash flooding, with a lower risk of strong storms \nproducing large hail and severe wind gusts. A Flood Watch remains\nin effect for northern Arizona through Tuesday evening.\n\nOn Wednesday, the low center moves into New Mexico, with chances\nfor showers and storms primarily remaining across eastern \nArizona. High pressure is forecast to bring a return of dry \nweather, with daytime temperatures warming to near average on \nThursday and into the weekend.",
